<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kronborg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1574">1574</a>–<a href="/years/1585">1585</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Kronborg Castle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kronborg is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a> on the narrowest point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> collected the Sound Toll from passing ships. Frederick II rebuilt an earlier fortress there between 1574 and 1585, and Christian IV restored it after a fire in 1629. Known as Elsinore in Shakespeare's Hamlet, it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kronborg.jpg" alt="Kronborg" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArildV · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kronborg_April_2026_10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The castle began as a fortress called Krogen, meaning the Hook, built in the early fifteenth century by the Danish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a>. He insisted that ships entering or leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a> pay sound dues, and built a strong fortress at the narrowest point of the strait to enforce them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> then held both shores, and with Helsingborg Castle opposite and a guard ship it could control all navigation.</p><p>Krogen stood on Ørekrog, a sandy tongue of land, and consisted of a square curtain wall with stone buildings inside. Large parts of its walls survive within the present castle, and Christian III added corner bastions in 1558 and 1559.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-seven-years-war">Northern Seven Years' War</a> ended in 1570, Frederick II extended the bastions to meet the improved power of artillery. The works, led by the Flemish architect Hans Hendrik van Paesschen, were completed in 1577, and the castle then took the name Kronborg, Crown Castle. Its buildings were extended into three wings between 1574 and 1585. From 1578 Anthonis van Obbergen took over as master builder, and the south wing gained a storey and a ballroom measuring 62 by 12 metres, the largest hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-europe">Northern Europe</a> when finished in 1582.</p><p>The walls were clad in sandstone from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> and the roof in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> stayed there in 1590 after marrying Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. A fire on the night of 24 to 25 September 1629 destroyed much of the castle, sparing only the chapel. Christian IV rebuilt the exterior without major changes by 1639, though the interior never fully regained its former glory. During the war of 1658 to 1660 a Swedish army under Carl Gustaf Wrangel besieged and took Kronborg, and the Swedes carried off many art treasures, including the courtyard fountain.</p><p>The Crownwork and new ramparts followed in 1688 to 1690. From 1739 the castle also served as a prison, where Queen Caroline Mathilde was held in 1772. It was under military administration from 1785 to 1922, and the army left in 1923.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Kronborg is among the most important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> castles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-europe">Northern Europe</a> and was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2000. It was opened to the public in 1938 after a thorough renovation. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Elsinore</a>, the anglicised name of the surrounding town of Helsingør, it is the setting of Shakespeare's Hamlet, which was first staged at the castle in 1816 by soldiers of the garrison and has been performed there many times since. A legend also places the sleeping Danish hero Ogier the Dane, or Holger Danske, at Kronborg, waiting until his homeland needs him; a statue of him lies in the casemates.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kronborg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A separate Norwegian army was organised from 1628, and fortresses such as <b>Kronborg</b>, Akershus and Fredriksten guarded the realm.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Military</a></li></ul></section>
